#!/bin/sh
# w-fp-gate — "should PAM try the fingerprint this time?" for /etc/pam.d/polkit-1.
#
# Run by pam_exec as the FIRST auth module, purely so the next one can be skipped:
#
#   auth [success=ignore default=1] pam_exec.so quiet /usr/lib/w/w-fp-gate
#   auth sufficient                 pam_fprintd.so
#   auth include                    system-auth
#
#   exit 0     → success=ignore → carry on to pam_fprintd (the normal path)
#   exit != 0  → default=1      → jump over pam_fprintd, straight to the password
#
# ── Why this exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The W auth card offers "Use password" while it is waiting on the reader. Making
# that button immediate is impossible from outside PAM: pam_fprintd owns the
# conversation until its own max-tries/timeout expire (30s by default), so a
# password typed meanwhile just sits queued, and cancelling the session only
# restarts a stack that begins with pam_fprintd all over again. GDM solves the
# same problem with two PAM services (gdm-fingerprint / gdm-password) and picks
# one; polkit cannot — polkit-agent-helper-1 hardcodes the service name
# "polkit-1". So the choice has to live INSIDE the one stack, and this is it.
#
# w-authd drops the flag when the user asks for the password, then restarts the
# PolkitAgent.Session: the new run skips the reader and asks for a password at
# once. The flag is removed when the prompt ends (and on daemon start, so a crash
# cannot leave fingerprint disabled).
#
# ── Where the flag lives, and why NOT in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ─────────────────────
# ⚠️ Since polkit 126 the PAM conversation does not run in a setuid helper forked
# by the agent. It runs in polkit-agent-helper@.service — a socket-activated
# system unit, as ROOT, hardened with ProtectHome=yes, PrivateTmp=yes and
# ProtectSystem=strict. ProtectHome hides /home, /root AND /run/user, so a flag
# under the user's own runtime dir is invisible here no matter who we run as:
# measured on a live reader, the gate saw nothing and the reader ran anyway.
# /run/w/fp (tmpfiles.d/w.conf, sticky 1777) is outside that mask and readable.
#
# For the same reason the caller's uid tells us nothing — we ARE root now — so the
# account being authenticated comes from PAM itself via $PAM_USER, which pam_exec
# exports.
#
# ── Why this is safe ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The flag can only ever REMOVE an authentication method, never add or bypass
# one: its single effect is that the password is demanded. Every failure mode
# lands on the same side — a missing, unreadable or non-executable gate makes
# pam_exec return an error, which `default=1` also turns into "skip the reader,
# ask for the password".
#
# Two guards keep the sticky directory from becoming a way to poke at root:
# symlinks are refused outright (so the flag can never redirect a root stat into
# a path the user cannot reach), and the flag counts only if its owner is the
# account being authenticated (so a second local user cannot switch off someone
# else's reader — the worst they could do is disable their own).
#
# Keep it trivial: a couple of stats, no network, no D-Bus, no loops. It sits in
# front of every privileged action on the machine, and a gate that blocks is a
# machine that cannot authenticate.

u=$(id -u "${PAM_USER:-}" 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
[ -n "$u" ] || exit 0

f="/run/w/fp/fp-skip-$u"
[ -h "$f" ] && exit 0
[ -f "$f" ] || exit 0
[ "$(stat -c %u "$f" 2>/dev/null)" = "$u" ] || exit 0

exit 1
